<div>
    <div class="mb-16">
        <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-white sm:text-5xl mb-6">
            <span class="bg-gradient-to-r from-emerald-400 via-cyan-400 to-amber-400 bg-clip-text text-transparent">Multiple Databases</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-8">
            One connection for the whole app, or several named connections in one process &mdash;
            including SoliDB, Postgres, MySQL, and SQLite together. Models choose with
            <code class="text-cyan-400">connection "name"</code>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-cyan-500/10 border border-cyan-500/20 mb-10">
        <p class="text-cyan-200 text-sm leading-relaxed">

            <strong class="text-white">Three modes:</strong>
            (1) default SoliDB via <code>SOLIDB_*</code>,
            (2) whole app on SQL with <code>SOLI_DB_ADAPTER</code> + <code>DATABASE_URL</code>,
            (3) multi-DB via <code>config/database.toml</code> + per-model binding.
            Design matrix: <code>docs/sql-adapter-design.md</code>.
            Narrative: <a href="/docs/blog/multi-database" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">blog post</a>.
            Per-adapter notes:
            <a href="/docs/database/postgres" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">PostgreSQL</a>,
            <a href="/docs/database/mysql" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">MySQL</a>,
            <a href="/docs/database/sqlite" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">SQLite</a>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="single" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Single connection (no TOML)</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Without <code>config/database.toml</code>, Soli builds one connection named
        <code class="text-cyan-400">primary</code> from the environment.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Variable</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Purpose</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Default</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">SOLIDB_HOST</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">SoliDB URL</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-green-300">http://localhost:6745</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">SOLIDB_DATABASE</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">SoliDB database name</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-green-300">default</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">SOLI_DB_ADAPTER</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>solidb</code>, <code>postgres</code>, <code>mysql</code>, <code>sqlite</code>, or <code>d1</code> (Cloudflare Workers &mdash; see <a href="/docs/development-tools/edge#d1" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">D1</a>)</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-green-300">solidb</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">DATABASE_URL</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Required for SQL adapters; for <code>d1</code>, the binding (<code>d1://DB</code>, the default); on the edge build, <code>postgres</code> defaults to <code>hyperdrive://HYPERDRIVE</code> (<a href="/docs/development-tools/edge#hyperdrive" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Hyperdrive</a>)</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-gray-500">unset</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">SOLI_DB_POOL_SIZE</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">SQL pool size</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-green-300">10</code></td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">.env</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm"># SoliDB (default)
SOLIDB_HOST=http://localhost:6745
SOLIDB_DATABASE=myapp

# Or: whole app on Postgres document tables
# SOLI_DB_ADAPTER=postgres
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@localhost:5432/myapp</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="toml" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24"><code>config/database.toml</code></h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Loaded at <code>soli serve</code> / migrate / import after <code>.env</code>.
        Secrets stay in env via <code>${VAR}</code> / <code>${VAR:-default}</code> expansion.
    </p>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-8">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">config/database.toml</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-toml text-sm">default = "primary"

[connections.primary]
adapter = "solidb"
host = "${SOLIDB_HOST:-http://localhost:6745}"
database = "${SOLIDB_DATABASE:-default}"
username = "${SOLIDB_USERNAME:-}"
password = "${SOLIDB_PASSWORD:-}"

[connections.legacy]
adapter = "postgres"
url = "${LEGACY_DATABASE_URL}"
pool = 10

[connections.warehouse]
adapter = "mysql"
url = "${WAREHOUSE_DATABASE_URL}"
pool = 5

[connections.analytics]
adapter = "sqlite"
url = "sqlite://db/analytics.sqlite3"   # a path, not a server</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Field reference</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Field</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Adapters</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Description</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">default</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">&mdash;</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Connection for models without <code>connection "…"</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">adapter</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">all</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>solidb</code>, <code>postgres</code>, <code>mysql</code>, <code>sqlite</code>, or <code>d1</code> (Cloudflare Workers &mdash; see <a href="/docs/development-tools/edge#d1" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">D1</a>)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">host</code> / <code class="text-amber-300">database</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">solidb</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">SoliDB URL and database name</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">url</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">postgres, mysql, sqlite</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Connection string, or a SQLite path (<code>sqlite://db/app.sqlite3</code>) &mdash; <strong class="text-white">required</strong> for SQL</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">pool</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">postgres, mysql, sqlite</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Pool size (default 10; 5 on SQLite, 1 for <code>:memory:</code>)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">replicas</code> / <code class="text-amber-300">replica</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">postgres, mysql</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Read replica URLs (an array, or one string) &mdash; see <a href="#replicas" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Read replicas</a></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-amber-300">replica_delay</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">postgres, mysql</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Seconds a client that wrote keeps reading the primary (default 2)</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-amber-500/5 border border-amber-500/20 mb-8">
        <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">
            <strong class="text-amber-300">SoliDB connections and env:</strong> SoliDB requests
            are always routed to the env-configured <code>SOLIDB_HOST</code> /
            <code>SOLIDB_DATABASE</code>, so a <code>solidb</code> connection's
            <code>host</code>/<code>database</code> must match the env values (use the
            <code>${SOLIDB_HOST:-…}</code> expansion shown above). A mismatch is rejected at
            boot rather than silently sending traffic to the wrong server. Per-connection
            SoliDB targets (two SoliDB servers/databases in one app) are not supported yet;
            secondary connections must use a SQL adapter.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-white/5 border border-white/10 mb-12">
        <p class="text-gray-400 text-sm">
            <strong class="text-white">Precedence:</strong> TOML present &rarr; named registry.
            No TOML &rarr; env-only <code>primary</code> (unchanged for existing apps).
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="model" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Per-model <code>connection</code></h2>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-8">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">app/models/*.sl</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">class User &lt; Model
  # uses default ("primary")
end

class LegacyOrder &lt; Model
  connection "legacy"
end

class FactSale &lt; Model
  connection "warehouse"
end</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <li>String or symbol name; validated at class load against the registry</li>
        <li>STI subclasses inherit the parent connection unless redeclared</li>
        <li>CRUD and the query builder route through the active connection</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="sql" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">SQL document backends</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        Postgres, MySQL, and SQLite connections store each collection as
        <code class="text-cyan-400">_key</code> + <code class="text-cyan-400">doc</code>
        (JSONB, JSON, or TEXT read with SQLite's json1 functions).
        Hash-style filters only &mdash; not raw SDBQL.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Capability</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">SQL docs</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">CRUD, hash <code>.where</code> (equality, comparisons, IN, LIKE, OR), order/limit/count</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">sum / avg / min / max, <code>delete_all</code> / <code>update_all</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Batched <code>.includes</code> (belongs_to / has_many / has_one)</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Relation readers (<code>owner.posts</code> and its chains, <code>post.author</code>, has_one, HABTM) and <code>dependent:</code> cascades</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ (after 2.24.0; they raised on SQL before)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Multi-row <code>group_by</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Batched HABTM <code>.includes</code> + <code>includes_count</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">(two queries: the join table, then the targets)</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>index</code> declarations / <code>soli db:indexes</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">expression index on the JSON field (generated column on MySQL)</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Atomic <code>increment</code> / <code>decrement</code> / counter caches</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">one arithmetic UPDATE (no <code>_rev</code>, no retry loop)</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>Model.find_by_sql(sql, binds?)</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">raw SELECT escape hatch, positional binds</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>create_many</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">one multi-row INSERT per 500-row chunk, in one transaction</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>pluck</code> / <code>select</code> on column models</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">pushed into the SELECT list</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>through:</code> includes</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">three batched queries via the intermediate model</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>.join</code> (relation existence filter)</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">correlated EXISTS, so parents are not duplicated</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>.having</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">one comparison of a group key or aggregate alias against a number</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Dev bar / <code>dev_queries()</code> / N+1 detection</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">the SQL, its binds, and its duration are logged per request</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">TLS to the server</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">Postgres / MySQL, opportunistic by default and verifiable on request &mdash; see <a href="/docs/database/postgres#tls" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Postgres</a> and <a href="/docs/database/mysql#tls" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">MySQL</a> (SQLite is a local file, so there is no connection to encrypt)</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>soli db:create</code> / <code>soli db:drop</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">(SoliDB creates its database on first use instead)</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>soli db:schema:dump</code> / <code>db:schema:load</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ <span class="text-gray-500">writes / applies db/schema.sql plus applied migration versions</span></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>Model.transaction</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-green-400">✓ (holds one pool connection for the block)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Graph, vector, columnar, timeseries</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-red-400">✗ SoliDB-only</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Raw SDBQL / string <code>.where("doc&hellip;")</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4 text-red-400">✗ SoliDB-only <span class="text-gray-500">(use the hash form, or <code>find_by_sql</code>)</span></td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Import SoliDB &rarr; SQL</h3>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-12">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">SOLI_DB_ADAPTER=postgres DATABASE_URL=postgres://…
SOLIDB_HOST=… SOLIDB_USERNAME=… SOLIDB_PASSWORD=…
soli db:import              # all non-_ collections
soli db:import posts users  # named only</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="connection-pool" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Connection pool</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Each SQL connection keeps a pool of up to <code>pool</code> connections (<code>SOLI_DB_POOL_SIZE</code>; 10, or 5 on SQLite),
        opened as requests need them. The first opens when the pool is created, so a wrong URL or an unreachable
        server fails at boot rather than on the first query, and a refused connection reports the driver&rsquo;s error
        at once. When every connection is in use, a query waits up to 10 seconds for one to come back.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        A connection is handed out without a check when it was last used under half a second ago. One that sat idle
        longer, or whose last query failed, is pinged first and replaced if it is dead &mdash; so a busy app pays no
        validation round trip, and a database restart costs at most one failed query per stale connection.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Two more round trips are saved per query:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 space-y-2 mb-12">
        <li><strong class="text-white">Statements are prepared once per connection</strong> (Postgres, SQLite), not once per query.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">A table known to exist is read directly.</strong> A read on a table that may not exist answers empty
            (<code>find_by</code> &rarr; <code>nil</code>, <code>where</code> &rarr; <code>[]</code>); the first read checks, later ones skip the check.
            A table dropped from outside the app still reads as empty: the error is recognised and the check comes back for
            that table. Inside a transaction the check always runs.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="sqlite" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">SQLite specifics</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        The dedicated <a href="/docs/database/sqlite" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">SQLite</a>
        page covers URL forms, WAL, jobs, backups, and the numeric-affinity caveat.
        The short version: SQLite is a file, not a server &mdash; the shortest path from
        <code class="text-cyan-400">soli new</code> to persistent data, and a good fit for
        single-node apps, embedded and desktop builds, CI, and tests.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">URL forms</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">URL</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Meaning</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-green-300">sqlite://db/app.sqlite3</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Relative path (the directory is created if missing)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-green-300">sqlite:///var/lib/app/app.db</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Absolute path</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-green-300">sqlite:app.db</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Path, short form</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-green-300">sqlite::memory:</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Private in-memory database, gone at exit</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-8">
        Every connection is opened in <strong class="text-white">WAL</strong> mode with a
        10-second busy timeout and foreign keys on.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">What to know before you choose it</h3>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 space-y-2 text-sm mb-8">
        <li><strong class="text-white">One writer at a time.</strong> WAL lets readers run during a write, but writers serialize. A busy write path across many processes belongs on Postgres.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">No exact numeric type.</strong> A <code class="text-cyan-400">DECIMAL</code>/<code class="text-cyan-400">NUMERIC</code> column has NUMERIC <em>affinity</em>, not an exact type: SQLite converts the value to a <code class="text-cyan-400">REAL</code>, so a stored <code class="text-cyan-400">19.90</code> reads back as <code class="text-cyan-400">19.9</code>, and a value beyond f64's exact range loses precision on write. Postgres <code class="text-cyan-400">numeric</code> and MySQL <code class="text-cyan-400">decimal</code> keep the scale. Declare the column <code class="text-cyan-400">TEXT</code> if exact decimal text matters.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Backups are file copies</strong> &mdash; use <code class="text-cyan-400">.backup</code> or <code class="text-cyan-400">VACUUM INTO</code>, never a <code class="text-cyan-400">cp</code> of a live WAL database.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white"><code class="text-cyan-400">:memory:</code> is one connection.</strong> The pool is forced to a single connection, because a second one would be a second, empty database.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-cyan-500/10 border border-cyan-500/20 mb-12">
        <p class="text-cyan-200 text-sm leading-relaxed">

            <strong class="text-white">Background jobs work unchanged.</strong>
            Postgres claims with <code>SKIP LOCKED</code> and MySQL with a claim token;
            SQLite takes the database write lock (<code>BEGIN IMMEDIATE</code>) for the
            length of the claim, which is exclusive by construction. Leases, retries, and
            cron behave the same &mdash; see
            <a href="/docs/builtins/jobs" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Background Jobs</a>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="rules" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Cross-connection rules</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Operation</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Rule</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>.includes</code> across connections</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><strong class="text-white">Error</strong> (both names in the message)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">FK stored across DBs</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Allowed; no integrity guarantees</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>Model.transaction</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Begins on the receiving model's connection; covers that connection only</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="my-6 p-4 rounded-lg bg-yellow-500/10 border border-yellow-500/20 mb-12">
        <p class="text-yellow-200 text-sm">
            <strong class="text-white">No federated joins.</strong>
            Place related models on the same connection when you need eager loads.
            Distributed transactions are out of scope.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h2 id="columns" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Column-aware models (existing databases)</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        The document backend above stores every collection as <code class="text-cyan-400">_key</code> + <code class="text-cyan-400">doc</code>, so it only reads tables Soli created. To point Soli at a database that <strong class="text-white">already exists</strong> &mdash; a legacy app's schema, a warehouse table, anything with real columns &mdash; declare the physical table on the model.
    </p>

    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <label class="block px-4 py-2 bg-white/5 text-xs text-gray-400 font-mono border-b border-white/5">app/models/order.sl</label>
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">class Order &lt; Model
  connection "legacy"    # a postgres, mysql, or sqlite connection
  table "orders"         # bind to an existing table -&gt; column mode
end</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        <code class="text-cyan-400">table "…"</code> is what switches the model into <strong class="text-white">column mode</strong>: the model reads and writes the table's real columns, and never creates or alters it.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        The table can be one you already have, or one a <strong class="text-white">migration</strong> builds:
        <code class="text-cyan-400">create_table("orders", { … })</code> declares real columns portably across
        Postgres, MySQL, and SQLite, and the types it emits are the ones introspection reads back &mdash; see
        <a href="/docs/database/migrations#sql-adapters" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Migrations &rarr; On the SQL adapters</a>.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">What Soli learns, and when</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">At boot, Soli introspects each declared table once and caches the result (<code class="text-cyan-400">information_schema</code> on Postgres and MySQL, <code class="text-cyan-400">PRAGMA table_info</code> on SQLite):</p>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 space-y-2 text-sm mb-6">
        <li>every column, its type, and whether it is nullable;</li>
        <li>the <strong class="text-white">primary key</strong>, detected automatically &mdash; including whether the database generates it (<code class="text-cyan-400">BIGSERIAL</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">IDENTITY</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">AUTO_INCREMENT</code>), so inserts leave it to the database;</li>
        <li>whether <code class="text-cyan-400">created_at</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">updated_at</code> exist &mdash; they are stamped only if they do.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-8">
        Problems fail the boot with a message naming the connection and table: a missing table, a <strong class="text-white">composite</strong> primary key (not supported yet), no primary key at all, or a <code class="text-cyan-400">solidb</code> connection. Editing a model in <code class="text-cyan-400">--dev</code> re-introspects; an <code class="text-cyan-400">ALTER TABLE</code> while the server runs needs a restart.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Type mapping</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">PostgreSQL</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">MySQL</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">SQLite (declared)</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Soli</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>int2</code> / <code>int4</code> / <code>int8</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>smallint</code> … <code>bigint</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">any name with <code>INT</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Int</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>float4</code> / <code>float8</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>float</code> / <code>double</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>REAL</code>, <code>DOUBLE</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Float</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>numeric</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>decimal</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>DECIMAL</code>, <code>NUMERIC</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Float <span class="text-gray-500">(precision caveat)</span></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>bool</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>tinyint(1)</code>, <code>boolean</code>, <code>bit(1)</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>BOOLEAN</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Bool</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>text</code> / <code>varchar</code> / <code>citext</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>varchar</code> / <code>text</code> / <code>enum</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>TEXT</code>, <code>VARCHAR</code>, none</td><td class="py-3 px-4">String</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>uuid</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">&mdash;</td><td class="py-3 px-4">String</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>UUID</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">String</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>date</code>, <code>timestamptz</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>date</code>, <code>datetime</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>DATE</code>, <code>DATETIME</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">DateTime (native)</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>json</code> / <code>jsonb</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>json</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Hash / Array</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>JSON</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Hash / Array</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>bytea</code>, arrays, geometry</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>blob</code>, geometry, unsigned <code>bigint</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>BLOB</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4 text-red-400">unsupported</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 space-y-2 text-sm mb-8">
        <li><strong class="text-white">Exact numerics</strong> travel as text and read as Float, so a value beyond f64's exact range loses precision on read; writes keep their scale.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Unsupported columns</strong> are skipped on read and error clearly if you filter or write them &mdash; they never silently corrupt a row.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">MySQL stores no offset</strong>: <code class="text-cyan-400">datetime</code>/<code class="text-cyan-400">timestamp</code> values are interpreted as UTC.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">SQLite enforces no types.</strong> It applies affinity to the declared type, so any column can hold any value. Soli reads the declared type to decide how to convert, and reads each value by what it actually is &mdash; a <code class="text-cyan-400">DATETIME</code> holding a unix timestamp becomes RFC 3339, exactly like a stored text date.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">A SQLite <code class="text-cyan-400">INTEGER PRIMARY KEY</code> is generated</strong> (it aliases the rowid, with or without <code class="text-cyan-400">AUTOINCREMENT</code>), so inserts omit it. A key of any other type must be supplied.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Supported operations</h3>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-8">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">order = Order.find(42)                       # real primary key, Int or String
Order.find_by("email", "a@b.c")              # equality on a real column
Order.where({ "status": "open" }).all
Order.where({ "assignee_id": null }).all     # compiles to IS NULL
Order.where({ "status": "open" }).order("created_at", "desc").limit(20).all
Order.where({ "status": "open" }).count
Order.where({ "status": "open" }).exists
Order.sum("total").all                       # sum/avg/min/max on numeric columns
Order.create({ "name": "Ada", "total": "19.99" })
order.status = "closed"
order.save()
order.delete()
Order.transaction(fn() { ... })              # real SQL transaction</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        <code class="text-cyan-400">pluck</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">select</code> are
        <strong class="text-white">pushed into the SELECT list</strong> on column models, so a
        projection reads two columns instead of fifty on a wide table; the primary key is always
        included so the row stays identifiable. A field that is not a real column (a nested path, a
        computed alias) falls back to the client-side projection rather than failing.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Raw SQL escape hatch</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">For a query the portable surface cannot express:</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-8">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># Positional binds: $1/$2 on Postgres, ? on MySQL and SQLite.
Order.find_by_sql("SELECT * FROM orders WHERE total &gt; ? AND status = ?", [100, "open"])

# A single `doc` column hydrates as documents (document tables);
# any other shape becomes a hash per row.
Post.find_by_sql("SELECT doc FROM posts WHERE doc-&gt;&gt;'slug' = $1", ["hello"])</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-8">
        Values are <strong class="text-white">bound</strong>, never interpolated, so a value that
        looks like SQL stays a value. <code class="text-cyan-400">find_by_sql</code> is SQL-only; on
        SoliDB it raises and points at <code class="text-cyan-400">Model.query</code> with SDBQL.
    </p>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Also supported</h3>

    <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-gray-400 space-y-2 text-sm mb-8">

        <li><strong class="text-white">Batched eager loading</strong> &mdash; <code class="text-cyan-400">belongs_to</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">has_many</code>, <code class="text-cyan-400">has_one</code>, HABTM, <code class="text-cyan-400">through:</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">includes_count</code>, over the real foreign-key columns. A hash filter on <code class="text-cyan-400">.includes("rel", { "visible": true })</code> applies to the related rows. A parent with no children gets <code class="text-cyan-400">[]</code>, never null.</li>

        <li><strong class="text-white"><code class="text-cyan-400">group_by</code></strong> with sum/avg/min/max/count over real columns, plus <code class="text-cyan-400">.having("n &gt; 5")</code> and <code class="text-cyan-400">.join("comments")</code> (a correlated <code>EXISTS</code>).</li>

        <li><strong class="text-white"><code class="text-cyan-400">delete_all</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">update_all</code></strong> &mdash; bulk writes that skip validations and callbacks (as on the document path) but still stamp <code class="text-cyan-400">updated_at</code> when the table has it, and never rewrite the primary key.</li>

        <li><strong class="text-white">Atomic <code class="text-cyan-400">increment</code> / <code class="text-cyan-400">decrement</code> and counter caches</strong> &mdash; one arithmetic <code>UPDATE</code> on the column.</li>

        <li><strong class="text-white"><code class="text-cyan-400">soft_delete</code></strong>, provided the table has a <code class="text-cyan-400">deleted_at</code> column. Without one there is nowhere to record the deletion, so boot fails with that message rather than silently returning deleted rows.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white"><code class="text-cyan-400">encrypts</code></strong>, provided each named field is a string/text column. Values are AES-256-GCM ciphertext at rest and plaintext on the instance after <code>create</code> / <code>save</code> / <code>find</code>. Boot fails if the column is missing or not text.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">STI</strong> (<code>class Admin &lt; User</code> with <code>table</code> on the base). Subclass writes stamp a <code>type</code> string column; subclass queries add <code>type IN (class, descendants)</code>. Boot fails if an STI subclass's table has no <code>type</code> column.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-8">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
            <pre><code class="language-soli text-sm">class Person &lt; Model
  table "people"         # id, name, ssn, type, timestamps
  encrypts(:ssn)
end

class Admin &lt; Person
end

a = Admin.create({ "name": "Root", "ssn": "000-00-0001" })
a.ssn                    # plaintext
Admin.find_by("name", "Root").type   # "Admin"
Person.where({ "name": "Root" }).count()  # 1 — base matches every type</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3 class="text-lg font-semibold text-white mb-4">Not supported on column-aware models</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">Each of these raises an error naming the feature rather than returning wrong data:</p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Feature</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Why</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Raw/string <code>.where("doc…")</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">SDBQL has no meaning against columns &mdash; use the hash form</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>.includes</code> across storage shapes</td><td class="py-3 px-4">Both models must be column-aware &mdash; matching a real column against a JSON field is not a join Soli will guess at</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>.join</code> on <code>belongs_to</code> / HABTM / <code>through:</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">Existence filter needs the child to hold the FK; use <code>.includes</code> and filter the related rows</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Composite primary keys</td><td class="py-3 px-4">Key handling is single-column throughout; refused at boot with the columns named</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>grouped {}</code>, graph, vector, columnar, timeseries</td><td class="py-3 px-4">SoliDB features</td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Auto-create / index sync / implicit <code>ALTER</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">A <strong class="text-white">model</strong> never issues DDL in column mode. A <a href="/docs/database/migrations#sql-adapters" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">migration</a> can create and alter column tables explicitly</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">Doc-store models on the same connection keep working exactly as before; column mode is per model.</p>

    <h2 id="hybrid" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Hybrid example</h2>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># Controllers use the same Model API
def index
  @users  = User.limit(20).all
  @orders = LegacyOrder.where({ "status": "open" }).limit(20).all
  render("home/index")
end

# Raises — different connections
# LegacyOrder.includes("user").all</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2 id="tenants" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">One database per tenant</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        A SaaS keeps its accounts and sites in one database and each site's data in a database of its own. In Soli that is two connections: the main one, and one whose <code>url</code> &mdash; or, on SoliDB, <code>database</code> &mdash; holds <code class="text-cyan-400">{tenant}</code>:
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-toml text-sm"># config/database.toml
default = "primary"

[connections.primary]            # accounts, sites, jobs, sessions
adapter = "postgres"
url = "${DATABASE_URL}"

[connections.site]               # one database per site
adapter = "postgres"
url = "postgres://app@db.internal/site_{tenant}"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        The same works on every adapter: <code>mysql://…/site_{tenant}</code>, <code>sqlite://db/sites/{tenant}.sqlite3</code> (one file per site), <code>database = "site_{tenant}"</code> on SoliDB, <code>d1://SITE_{tenant}</code> on <a href="/docs/development-tools/edge#d1" class="text-cyan-400 hover:underline">Cloudflare D1</a> (one binding per site), and on the edge <code>do://SITES/{tenant}</code> with the D1 adapter: a <a href="/docs/development-tools/edge#durable-objects" class="text-cyan-400 hover:underline">Durable Object per site</a>, created by naming it, with nothing declared per site. The models of the site data name the connection; the others stay on the main database:
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># app/models/site.sl — main database
class Site &lt; Model
end

# app/models/post.sl — the current site's database
class Post &lt; Model
  connection "site"
end</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">A request chooses its site, usually in a middleware, from the host or a path segment:</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># app/middleware/site.sl
# order: 10
def choose_site(req)
  site = Site.find_by("host", req["headers"]["host"])
  halt(404, "Unknown site") if site.nil?

  switch_tenant(site.db_key)
  req
end</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        From then on every query on <code>site</code> goes to <code>site_&lt;db_key&gt;</code> &mdash; <code>Post.all</code>, <code>Post.create(…)</code>, query builders, <code>grouped</code>, transactions &mdash; and the main database answers the rest.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-8">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Call</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Does</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>switch_tenant(key)</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">The tenant for the rest of the request (or script). <code>switch_tenant(nil)</code> clears it.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>switch_tenant(key, {"url": u})</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">A site whose data lives elsewhere &mdash; another server, another credential. <code>{"database": name}</code> on SoliDB. The URL replaces the template for that site.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>with_tenant(key) do … end</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">A block under that tenant (an admin page reading several sites, a script, a console); the tenant before comes back afterwards, also when the block raises. Takes the same options hash.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code>current_tenant()</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">The current key, or <code>nil</code>.</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-8">
        <li><strong class="text-white">The key goes into URLs, file names and database names</strong>, so it is held to 1&ndash;63 letters, digits, <code>_</code> and <code>-</code>, starting with a letter or digit. Anything else raises &mdash; a key taken from a host name cannot reach another directory or database. Store a key on the site row rather than using the host itself.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">No tenant, no data.</strong> A query on a tenant connection before <code>switch_tenant</code> fails with an error saying so; it never falls back to another site's database. Each request starts and ends with no tenant, so a worker that last served one site cannot answer the next request with its data &mdash; WebSocket and LiveView events start with none too.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Jobs run under the tenant they were enqueued under</strong>, with the explicit URL if the site had one (stored in the job row, in the main database). Cron jobs have no tenant: loop over the sites with <code>with_tenant</code>.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Keep the default connection the main database.</strong> Jobs, sessions and the error tracker live on the default connection; a default connection with <code>{tenant}</code> in it would scatter them across sites.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Pools.</strong> Each site's database gets its own pool. Soli keeps at most <code>SOLI_TENANT_POOLS</code> (32) site pools open per adapter and closes the oldest when one more is needed; that site's next request reopens it. Set <code>pool</code> on the <code>site</code> connection to size each one.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">SQLite</strong> site files rely on SQLite's automatic checkpoints, without the background checkpointer the main file gets. <strong class="text-white">SoliDB</strong> creates a site's database on its first write.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Column-aware models</strong> (<code>table "orders"</code>) on a tenant connection are inspected on first use rather than at boot, and the schema read for the first site serves the others: every site runs the same migrations.</li>
        <li><code>switch_tenant</code> inside a transaction raises (the transaction holds the current site's database), and reads a <code>grouped</code> block deferred run before the tenant changes.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3 id="tenant-migrations" class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Creating and migrating a site's database</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">A site's database is created explicitly &mdash; when the site is created, not on its first request:</p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli db:create --tenant acme          # CREATE DATABASE site_acme (SQLite: the file)
soli db:migrate up --tenant acme      # the migrations that declare connection "site"
soli db:migrate status --tenant acme
soli db:drop --tenant acme</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        Migrations name their connection (<code>connection "site"</code> as their first line). A plain <code>soli db:migrate up</code> runs the main database's and reports the site ones it left for <code>--tenant</code>; <code>--tenant KEY</code> runs only the site ones, on that site's database, which keeps its own <code>_migrations</code> table. Migrating every site is a loop over the keys. <code>--tenant</code> is refused on a connection without <code>{tenant}</code>, so it cannot act on the main database by mistake.
    </p>

    <h2 id="replicas" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Read replicas</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        A Postgres or MySQL connection can name read replicas. Reads go to them under the <strong class="text-white"><code>reading</code></strong> role; writes always go to the primary.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-toml text-sm">[connections.primary]
adapter = "postgres"
url = "${DATABASE_URL}"
replicas = ["${REPLICA_URL:-}"]   # or replica = "…"; several are spread across requests
replica_delay = 2                 # seconds a client that wrote keeps reading the primary</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        An entry that expands to an empty string is ignored, so <code>${REPLICA_URL:-}</code> turns replicas off in an environment that has none. <code>replicas</code> on a SQLite, D1 or SoliDB connection is refused at boot, and so is any replica on the edge (Cloudflare Workers) build.
    </p>

    <h3 id="replica-roles" class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Which role a query runs under</h3>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Where</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Role</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">A <code>GET</code> or <code>HEAD</code> request</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>reading</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">The same, from a client that wrote less than <code>replica_delay</code> seconds ago</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>writing</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Any other request (<code>POST</code>, <code>PATCH</code>, <code>DELETE</code>, …)</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>writing</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Jobs, socket events, the console, migrations, scripts</td><td class="py-3 px-4"><code>writing</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td class="py-3 px-4">Inside <code>with_role("reading") do … end</code> / <code>with_role("writing") do … end</code></td><td class="py-3 px-4">the one named</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Under <code>reading</code>, only the reads of a model or query go to a replica: <code>all</code>, <code>find</code>, <code>find_by</code>, <code>where</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>exists</code>, the aggregates, <code>group_by</code>, eager loads, and <code>find_by_sql</code> with a statement that starts with <code>SELECT</code>. Everything else stays on the primary whatever the role: writes, DDL, migrations, <code>db.execute</code>, a <code>find_by_sql</code> that starts with <code>WITH</code>, and every read inside a transaction.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-soli text-sm"># GET /reports/monthly — a GET reads a replica already
def monthly(req)
  @orders = Order.where({ month: params["month"] }).all
end

# Reads that must see the latest data, even on a GET
with_role("writing") do
  @balance = Account.find(id).balance
end

# A slow report from a POST or a job, kept off the primary
with_role("reading") do
  @rows = Event.where({ kind: "signup" }).count
end

current_role()   # "reading" or "writing"</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h3 id="replica-lag" class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-4 scroll-mt-24">Reading what you wrote</h3>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">A replica lags the primary, so two rules keep a client from reading data older than its own writes:</p>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-4">
        <li><strong class="text-white">Within a request</strong>, the first write sends the request's later reads to the primary. A <code>GET</code> that writes (a view counter, a <code>last_seen_at</code>) reads the primary from then on.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Across requests</strong>, a request that wrote sets a <code>soli_db_primary</code> cookie (<code>HttpOnly</code>, <code>SameSite=Lax</code>, lasting <code>replica_delay</code> seconds), and that client's <code>GET</code>s run under <code>writing</code> until it expires &mdash; so the page a <code>POST</code> redirects to shows what was just saved. The cookie is not signed: forging it only sends that client's own reads to the primary.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        <strong class="text-white">A record read from a replica is saved field by field.</strong> Once a request has read a replica, <code>save</code> and <code>update</code> on a record write only the fields that changed since it was loaded, plus the ones <code>update(…)</code> / <code>save(…)</code> was given &mdash; not the whole record, whose other fields may be older than the primary's. A <code>GET</code> that loads a post and bumps its view counter therefore leaves a title edited a second ago alone. An in-place change to a nested Hash or Array is not tracked: reassign the field (<code>post.meta = meta</code>) in such a request so it is written. Requests that read no replica write whole records, as before.
    </p>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-6">
        An explicit <code>with_role("reading")</code> block reads a replica even after a write: you asked for it. Unlike Rails, a write under <code>reading</code> is not refused &mdash; it goes to the primary.
    </p>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <li><strong class="text-white">Picking a replica.</strong> Each request reads one replica, taken in turn across requests. A replica that does not answer fails its reads; it is not taken out of rotation on its own (put a proxy such as PgBouncer or ProxySQL in front if you need failover).</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Pools.</strong> Each replica gets its own pool, sized by the connection's <code>pool</code>.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">One database per tenant.</strong> Replica URLs may hold <code>{tenant}</code> too (<code>replicas = ["postgres://app@replica/site_{tenant}"]</code>); a tenant switched to with its own <code>url</code> reads its primary.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white"><code>grouped</code> blocks</strong> keep their reads under the role they were made under: <code>with_role</code> runs a block's deferred reads before the role changes.</li>
        <li><strong class="text-white">Seeing it.</strong> Under <code>--dev</code>, the dev bar's DB panel shows the request's role in its header and tags each query <code>replica</code> or <code>primary</code>; <code>dev_queries()</code> entries carry <code>replica: true/false</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="migrations" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Migrations</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Default migrate target is the <strong class="text-white">default</strong> connection.
        Target a named SQL secondary with:
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-6">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm">soli db:migrate up --connection legacy
soli db:migrate status -c legacy
soli db:migrate down --connection legacy
soli db:migrate up --tenant acme      # one database per tenant: see above</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        Prefer naming the connection in migration filenames when multi-DB is in use.
        <code class="text-cyan-400">Model.transaction</code> works on Postgres, MySQL, and SQLite (one held pool connection for the block).
    </p>

    <h2 id="limits" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Limitations</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-gray-400 mb-12">
        <li>Per-connection SoliDB hosts: registry stores fields; prefer one SoliDB endpoint + SQL secondaries for now. A SoliDB connection with one database per tenant (<code>database = "site_{tenant}"</code>) is the exception: its database follows the tenant, on the <code>SOLIDB_HOST</code> server.</li>
        <li>A string-form <code>.includes("rel", "…")</code> filter is SoliDB-only; use a hash on SQL</li>
        <li>Read replicas: no automatic failover or replication-lag measurement; sharding (several primaries for one connection) is not supported</li>
        <li>pgvector on document tables remains SoliDB-only</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="compile-time" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Compile-time features</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-4">
        Postgres, MySQL, and SQLite client code is optional at <strong class="text-white">build time</strong> (all on by default).
        A binary built without <code class="text-cyan-400">postgres</code> or <code class="text-cyan-400">mysql</code>
        cannot open those adapters &mdash; boot fails with a rebuild hint if
        <code class="text-cyan-400">SOLI_DB_ADAPTER</code> or <code class="text-cyan-400">database.toml</code>
        selects one that was not compiled in.
    </p>
    <div class="rounded-lg bg-[#171412] overflow-hidden mb-4">
        <div class="p-4 overflow-x-auto">
<pre><code class="language-bash text-sm"># SoliDB only (no SQL client crates)
cargo install --path . --locked --no-default-features \
  --features embedding,llm,codegraph

# Postgres only (no MySQL, no SQLite)
cargo install --path . --locked --no-default-features \
  --features embedding,llm,codegraph,postgres</code></pre>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-gray-400 mb-12">
        Full table:
        <a href="/docs/getting-started/configuration#slim-binary-cargo-features" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Configuration &rarr; Slim binary</a>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="troubleshooting" class="text-2xl font-bold text-white mb-6 scroll-mt-24">Troubleshooting</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-12">
        <table class="w-full text-sm">
            <thead>
                <tr class="border-b border-white/10">
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Symptom</th>
                    <th class="text-left py-3 px-4 text-gray-400 font-semibold">Check</th>
                </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody class="divide-y divide-white/5 text-gray-400">
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">Unknown database connection "…"</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Name matches <code>[connections.*]</code> / <code>default</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">url</code> required for postgres/mysql/sqlite</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Set <code>url =</code> or expand <code>${…}</code> from <code>.env</code></td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4"><code class="text-cyan-400">not compiled into this soli binary</code></td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Rebuild with <code>--features postgres</code>, <code>mysql</code>, and/or <code>sqlite</code>
                        (<a href="/docs/getting-started/configuration#slim-binary-cargo-features" class="text-amber-400 hover:underline">Slim binary</a>)</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Includes error across DBs</td>
                    <td class="py-3 px-4">Expected &mdash; query each side separately or colocate models</td>
                </tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </div>

